var documenterSearchIndex = {"docs":
[{"category":"section","location":"generated/guide/#Usage-guide","page":"Guide","text":"This guide collapses the order parameter of two-dimensional site percolation, estimates the critical exponents with error bars, and tests one of them against its exact value. The data are the example of autoScale: the relative size y of the largest cluster, with its error, at occupation probability p, for system sizes L = 128 256 512.\n\nusing Autoscalers\nusing CairoMakie\nusing DelimitedFiles\nusing Random\n\ndir = joinpath(pkgdir(Autoscalers), \"test\", \"data\", \"autoscale\")\nread_size(L) = readdlm(joinpath(dir, \"orderParam_L$L.dat\"); comments=true)\ndata = [\n    (; L, p, y, y_err) for L in (128, 256, 512) for (p, y, y_err) in eachrow(read_size(L))\n]\nfirst(data, 3)\n3-element Vector{@NamedTuple{L::Int64, p::Float64, y::Float64, y_err::Float64}}:\n (L = 128, p = 0.514, y = 0.023437505, y_err = 6.8991e-5)\n (L = 128, p = 0.519, y = 0.026381917, y_err = 8.0497e-5)\n (L = 128, p = 0.524, y = 0.030066433, y_err = 9.5605e-5)\n\nAny Tables.jl table works, e.g. a vector of named tuples as here, or a DataFrame.","title":"Usage guide"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/guide/#Fitting-the-collapse","page":"Guide","text":"Near the percolation threshold p_c, finite-size scaling predicts y = L^-βν f(L^1ν (p - p_c)). With a = 1ν and b = βν, the data collapse onto f when L^b y is plotted against L^a (p - p_c):\n\nansatz = @ansatz (a, b, p_c) -> :L^b * (:y ± :y_err) ~ :L^a * (:p - p_c)\n@ansatz((a, b, p_c)->:L ^ b * (:y ± :y_err) ~ :L ^ a * (:p - p_c))\n\nThe scaling form holds close to p_c in the unscaled p, so the points that are scored are chosen with eval_window in p. It is narrow enough that every size covers it after scaling; otherwise fit_collapse would warn.\n\nscaler = Autoscaler(data, ansatz; eval_window=(0.583, 0.602))\nfit = fit_collapse(scaler, [0.7, 0.1, 0.59])\nfit.params\n3-element Vector{Float64}:\n 0.7494359072788891\n 0.10474307994821325\n 0.5926696656424555\n\nto_table gives the scaled data at the fitted parameters:\n\ntable = to_table(scaler, fit.params)\n\nfig = Figure(; size=(800, 350))\nunscaled = Axis(fig[1, 1]; xlabel=L\"p\", ylabel=L\"y\", title=\"unscaled\")\nscaled = Axis(fig[1, 2]; xlabel=L\"L^a (p - p_c)\", ylabel=L\"L^b y\", title=\"scaled\")\nvspan!(unscaled, 0.583, 0.602; color=(:gray, 0.2), label=\"eval_window\")\nfor size in (128, 256, 512)\n    rows = filter(r -> r.L == size, data)\n    p, y, y_err = getindex.(rows, :p), getindex.(rows, :y), getindex.(rows, :y_err)\n    errorbars!(unscaled, p, y, y_err)\n    scatter!(unscaled, p, y; markersize=6, label=\"L = $size\")\n    on_size = table.L .== size\n    errorbars!(scaled, table.x[on_size], table.y[on_size], table.y_err[on_size])\n    scatter!(scaled, table.x[on_size], table.y[on_size]; markersize=6)\nend\naxislegend(unscaled; position=:lt)\nfig\nFigure()","title":"Fitting the collapse"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/guide/#Error-bars","page":"Guide","text":"bootstrap refits the collapse to synthetic data drawn from the fitted master curve with the measured errors. Here n is small to keep the documentation fast; use at least 1000 replicates in practice.\n\nboot = bootstrap(scaler, fit.params; n=200, rng=Xoshiro(1))\nconfint(boot)\n(a = (0.7420773265835487, 0.7560098644017199), b = (0.10324529942444077, 0.1071137353897775), p_c = (0.5925780662486055, 0.5927395506776368))\n\nstderror(boot)\n(a = 0.003484665338465836, b = 0.0009901500941617808, p_c = 4.589912782455054e-5)\n\nconfint gives bias-corrected 95 % intervals, which can be asymmetric around the estimate, while stderror gives the standard deviation of the bootstrap samples.","title":"Error bars"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/guide/#Testing-an-exponent","page":"Guide","text":"For two-dimensional percolation ν = 43 exactly, so a = 34. The p-value of that hypothesis is\n\npvalue(boot, :a => 3 / 4)\n0.6557653194714023\n\nso the collapse is consistent with it. With method=:null, pvalue refits the collapse under the hypothesis instead. That is slower, but it can test several parameters at once, here both exact exponents, a = 34 and b = βν = 548:\n\npvalue(boot, :a => 3 / 4, :b => 5 / 48; method=:null, rng=Xoshiro(2))\n0.1691542288557214\n\nThe data are consistent with both.\n\n\n\nThis page was generated using Literate.jl.","title":"Testing an exponent"},{"category":"section","location":"internals/#Internals","page":"Internals","text":"Functions and types that are not part of the public interface, listed for contributors. They can change without notice.","title":"Internals"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#Autoscalers.bias_correction-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"Internals","text":"bias_correction(samples, estimate)\n\nThe bias correction z_0 = Φ^-1(P(θ^*  hatθ)) of the BC interval (Efron, 1987), counting ties as half. The fraction is kept within 1(2n) of 0 and 1, so that z_0 stays finite.\n\njulia> Autoscalers.bias_correction(1:9, 5) == 0  # centred: 4.5 of 9 below\ntrue\n\njulia> round(Autoscalers.bias_correction(1:9, 7); digits=3)  # 6.5 of 9 below\n0.589\n\njulia> round(Autoscalers.bias_correction(1:9, 100); digits=3)  # clamped at 1 - 1/18\n1.593\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Autoscalers.bias_correction"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#Autoscalers.error_dual-Union{Tuple{K}, Tuple{T}, Tuple{T, Any, Any, Any, Val{K}}} where {T, K}","page":"Internals","text":"error_dual(tag, value, (slot, errors), i, ::Val{K})\n\nvalue as a dual number whose slot-th of K partials is its error. Propagated through the ansatz, the partials of the scaled y are then σ_c  yc for each column c with errors, and their norm is the first-order error of the scaled y.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Autoscalers.error_dual"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#Autoscalers.extrapolate-NTuple{4, Any}","page":"Internals","text":"extrapolate(xs, ys, dys, x)\n\nLike interpolate(xs, ys, dys, x; sq_errs=true), but defined everywhere. Returns (y, dy2, d). Outside the data:\n\ny continues linearly from the end segment;\ndy2 is held at its value at the end;\nd is the distance outside the data range, in units of the range width (0 inside).\n\njulia> xs, ys, dys = [0.0, 1.0, 2.0], [0.0, 10.0, 20.0], [1.0, 1.0, 3.0];\n\njulia> Autoscalers.extrapolate(xs, ys, dys, 1.5)\n(15.0, 5.0, 0.0)\n\njulia> Autoscalers.extrapolate(xs, ys, dys, 3.0)  # half a range width beyond the end\n(30.0, 9.0, 0.5)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Autoscalers.extrapolate"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#Autoscalers.extrapolates-Tuple{Autoscaler, Any}","page":"Internals","text":"extrapolates(a::Autoscaler, args)\n\nWhether the fixed-window cost at the free parameters args relies on extrapolating curves beyond their data, i.e. has a non-zero penalty. Always false for the classic cost.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Autoscalers.extrapolates"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#Autoscalers.extrapolation_free_window-Tuple{Autoscaler, Any}","page":"Internals","text":"extrapolation_free_window(a::Autoscaler, params)\n\nAt the full parameters params, the number of scored points that need extrapolation, and the widest part of eval_window around its centre in which no point does (or nothing). A point needs extrapolation when its scaled x lies outside the data of another curve.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Autoscalers.extrapolation_free_window"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#Autoscalers.fixed_master_curve-Tuple{Autoscaler, Any, Any}","page":"Internals","text":"fixed_master_curve(a::Autoscaler, x, own_curve)\n\nError-weighted master curve at scaled x, built from all curves except own_curve (unless a.use_all_sizes), extrapolating where needed. Returns (; Y, dY2, penalty).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Autoscalers.fixed_master_curve"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#Autoscalers.fixed_window_breakdown-Tuple{Autoscaler, Any}","page":"Internals","text":"fixed_window_breakdown(a::Autoscaler, params)\n\nFixed-window cost, split into the collapse (χ²-like) part and the extrapolation penalty. Each curve contributes the mean over its scored points; the result is averaged over curves with at least one scored point.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Autoscalers.fixed_window_breakdown"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#Autoscalers.free_parameter_names-Tuple{Autoscaler}","page":"Internals","text":"free_parameter_names(a::Autoscaler)\n\nNames of the parameters that are not hardcoded, in the order the cost function expects them.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Autoscalers.free_parameter_names"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#Autoscalers.get_parameters!-Tuple{Autoscaler, Any}","page":"Internals","text":"get_parameters!(a::Autoscaler, args)\n\nLike get_parameters, but returns an internal buffer that is overwritten on the next call. Avoids an allocation per cost evaluation.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Autoscalers.get_parameters!"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#Autoscalers.get_parameters-Tuple{Autoscaler, Any}","page":"Internals","text":"get_parameters(a::Autoscaler, args)\n\nCombine the free parameters args with the hardcoded ones into the full parameter vector.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Autoscalers.get_parameters"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#Autoscalers.interpolate-NTuple{4, Any}","page":"Internals","text":"interpolate(x, y, y_err, selected_x; sq_errs=false)\n\nLinearly interpolate y and y_err between the knots x at selected_x. With sq_errs=true, the squared errors are interpolated and returned instead. Returns (y, y_err), or missing outside the range of x.\n\njulia> x, y, y_err = [0.0, 1.0, 2.0], [0.0, 10.0, 20.0], [1.0, 1.0, 3.0];\n\njulia> Autoscalers.interpolate(x, y, y_err, 1.5)\n(15.0, 2.0)\n\njulia> Autoscalers.interpolate(x, y, y_err, 1.5; sq_errs=true)  # mean of 1² and 3²\n(15.0, 5.0)\n\njulia> Autoscalers.interpolate(x, y, y_err, 3.0)\nmissing\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Autoscalers.interpolate"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#Autoscalers.lerp-Tuple{Any, Any, Any}","page":"Internals","text":"lerp((x1, y1), (x2, y2), x)\n\nLinearly interpolate (or extrapolate) at x on the line through (x1, y1) and (x2, y2).\n\njulia> Autoscalers.lerp((0.0, 1.0), (2.0, 3.0), 1.5)\n2.5\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Autoscalers.lerp"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#Autoscalers.model_data-Tuple{Autoscaler, Any}","page":"Internals","text":"model_data(a::Autoscaler, params; maxiter=50)\n\nData that lies exactly on the fitted collapse at the free parameters params. For each data point, the value in the first column with errors is replaced by the value whose scaled y equals the pooled_master_curve at that point. The ansatz is solved for it with Newton's method, which needs a single step when the scaled y is linear in that column.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Autoscalers.model_data"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#Autoscalers.parameter_tuple-Union{Tuple{P}, Tuple{Ansatz{P}, Any}} where P","page":"Internals","text":"parameter_tuple(a::Ansatz, params)\n\nThe values params as a NamedTuple keyed by the parameter names of a.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Autoscalers.parameter_tuple"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#Autoscalers.pooled_master_curve-Tuple{Autoscaler}","page":"Internals","text":"pooled_master_curve(a::Autoscaler)\n\nThe master curve at the scaled x of every data point: the error-weighted mean of all curves that cover it, including the point's own curve, so it is defined at every point. Uses the current scaling of a.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Autoscalers.pooled_master_curve"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#Autoscalers.raw_x-Tuple{Autoscaler}","page":"Internals","text":"raw_x(a::Autoscaler)\n\nUnscaled x of all data points, in the internal order.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Autoscalers.raw_x"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#Autoscalers.replicate!-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"Internals","text":"replicate!(f, n; rng, parallel, progress, desc)\n\nCall f(i, rng_i) for i in 1:n, each with its own random number generator seeded from rng, so the results do not depend on how the calls are scheduled over threads.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Autoscalers.replicate!"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#Autoscalers.rewrite-Tuple{Any, Autoscalers.AnsatzContext}","page":"Internals","text":"rewrite(ex, ctx::AnsatzContext)\n\nRewrite one side of an @ansatz into the body of f(row, p): columns :c become row.c, parameters p.name, $expr a value computed once, and every other name is resolved in the caller's module.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Autoscalers.rewrite"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#Autoscalers.scaled_y_and_slope-Tuple{Autoscaler, Vararg{Any, 4}}","page":"Internals","text":"scaled_y_and_slope(a::Autoscaler, i, p, column, value)\n\nScaled y of data point i at parameters p with column set to value, and its derivative with respect to that value.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Autoscalers.scaled_y_and_slope"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#Autoscalers.select_subset-Tuple{Autoscaler, Any, Any}","page":"Internals","text":"select_subset(c::Autoscaler, selected_x, selected_curve)\n\nFrom every curve other than selected_curve, select the two neighbouring points j and j + 1 with x_j  x_mathrmsel  x_j+1, where x_mathrmsel is selected_x, or nothing if that curve does not bracket it (Houdayer & Hartmann (2004), Appendix A).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Autoscalers.select_subset"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#Autoscalers.standard_cost_function-Tuple{Autoscaler, Any}","page":"Internals","text":"standard_cost_function(c::Autoscaler, params)\n\nQuality S of Eq. A1: the mean of (y - Y)^2  (δy^2 + δY^2) over all points for which the master curve Y  δY is defined. Inf if it is defined for none.\n\nReferences\n\nJ. Houdayer and A. K. Hartmann, Low-temperature behavior of two-dimensional Gaussian Ising spin glasses, Phys. Rev. B 70, 014418 (2004), doi:10.1103/PhysRevB.70.014418.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Autoscalers.standard_cost_function"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#Autoscalers.standard_master_curve-Tuple{Autoscaler, Any, Any}","page":"Internals","text":"standard_master_curve(c::Autoscaler, selected_x, selected_curve)\n\nWeighted linear fit through the neighbours of selected_x in all other curves, evaluated at selected_x (Eqs. A2 and A3). Returns (Y, dY2) or missing if no other curve brackets selected_x.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Autoscalers.standard_master_curve"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#Autoscalers.synthetic_data-Tuple{Any, Any, Autoscaler}","page":"Internals","text":"synthetic_data(rng, model, a::Autoscaler)\n\nmodel with Gaussian noise of the measured errors added to every column with errors.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Autoscalers.synthetic_data"},{"category":"section","location":"#Autoscalers.jl","page":"Home","text":"Automated finite-size scaling analysis (data collapse) in Julia.\n\nThe usage guide collapses example data, estimates its parameters with error bars and tests one of them against an expected value.","title":"Autoscalers.jl"},{"category":"section","location":"#Public-interface","page":"Home","text":"","title":"Public interface"},{"category":"section","location":"#Fitting-and-error-estimates","page":"Home","text":"","title":"Fitting and error estimates"},{"category":"macro","location":"#Autoscalers.@ansatz","page":"Home","text":"@ansatz (params...) -> lhs ~ rhs [size=:L] [x=:col] [group=:col]\n\nDefine a scaling Ansatz from a formula: the data collapse is sought for lhs (the scaled y) as a function of rhs (the scaled x).\n\nSyntax\n\n(params...) -> declares the parameters, in the order the Autoscaler expects their values. A single parameter can be written without parentheses.\n:col is a column of the data. Names that are not identifiers can be written as :var\"col name\".\n:col ± :col_err attaches the errors in column col_err to col. The left-hand side must contain at least one such column; their errors propagate into the scaled y.\n$expr inserts the value of expr once, when the ansatz is created.\nAny other name (a function, a constant like π, a variable) is looked up in the calling scope.\n\nOptions\n\nsize: column with the system size (default :L).\nx: column with the unscaled x, which the rows are sorted by and eval_window refers to. Only needed when the right-hand side uses more than one column besides size and group.\ngroup: further columns (one, or a tuple) that identify a curve together with size; see Ansatz.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> a = @ansatz (a, b, x_c) -> :L^b * (:y ± :y_err) ~ :L^a * (:x - x_c);\n\njulia> a.param_names, a.x, a.errors\n((:a, :b, :x_c), :x, (y = :y_err,))\n\njulia> a.f((; L=4, x=0.5, y=2.0), (; a=1.0, b=0.5, x_c=0.3))  # scaled (x, y)\n(0.8, 4.0)\n\nAn analytic background, a fixed exponent, and other column names:\n\njulia> ν = 0.75;\n\njulia> a = @ansatz (A, B, t_c) -> :N^0.1 * ((:E ± :dE) - A - B * (:t - t_c)) ~\n           :N^(1 / $ν) * (:t - t_c) size=:N;\n\njulia> a.size, a.x\n(:N, :t)\n\nOne curve per size and value of u, all sharing one master curve:\n\njulia> a = @ansatz (α, β, t_c) ->\n           :L^0.1 * ((:E ± :dE) - :u - α * :u^2 - β * (:t - t_c) * :u^2) ~\n           :L^1.5 * (:t - t_c) group=:u;\n\njulia> a.group\n(:u,)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Autoscalers.@ansatz"},{"category":"type","location":"#Autoscalers.Ansatz","page":"Home","text":"Ansatz(f, params; x, errors, columns=(), size=:L, group=())\n\nScaling ansatz given as a function f(row, p) that returns the scaled (x, y) of one data point. @ansatz builds the same from a formula.\n\nrow is a NamedTuple of the point's values in the columns size, group, x, the keys of errors and columns. p is a NamedTuple of the parameters.\n\nThe columns listed in errors are passed as ForwardDiff dual numbers that carry their errors, so the error of the scaled y follows from first-order error propagation (assuming independent errors). Therefore:\n\nf must accept any Real, not only Float64;\nthe scaled y must depend on at least one column with errors;\nthe scaled x must not depend on any column with errors.\n\nArguments\n\nparams: parameter names, in the order the Autoscaler expects their values.\nx: column with the unscaled x. Rows are sorted by it, and eval_window refers to it.\nerrors: NamedTuple mapping columns to their error columns, e.g. (; E=:dE).\ncolumns: further columns used by f.\nsize: column with the system size. Its values are passed as integers.\ngroup: further columns (a name or a tuple of names) that identify a curve together with size, e.g. group=:u when there is one curve per size and value of u. Each point is then compared with the master curve of all other curves. By default, each size is one curve. Group columns must be numeric, cannot be named like an output of to_table, and the data still needs at least two sizes.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> a = Ansatz((:a, :b, :t_c); x=:t, errors=(; E=:dE)) do row, p\n           return row.L^p.a * (row.t - p.t_c), row.L^p.b * row.E\n       end;\n\njulia> a.param_names\n(:a, :b, :t_c)\n\njulia> a.f((; L=4, t=0.5, E=2.0), (; a=1.0, b=0.5, t_c=0.3))  # scaled (x, y)\n(0.8, 4.0)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Autoscalers.Ansatz"},{"category":"type","location":"#Autoscalers.Autoscaler","page":"Home","text":"Autoscaler(\n    table, ansatz::Ansatz=DEFAULT_ANSATZ;\n    eval_window=nothing,\n    penalty=100.0,\n    classic_cost_function=false,\n    use_all_sizes=false,\n    fix=(;),\n)\n\nPrepare the data in table (any Tables.jl table) for a data collapse with ansatz. The default ansatz is @ansatz (a, b, x_c) -> :L^b * (:y ± :y_err) ~ :L^a * (:x - x_c).\n\nCalling the Autoscaler with values of the free parameters returns the cost of the collapse, which fit_collapse minimises. The parameters are those of ansatz.param_names, in that order, without the fixed ones.\n\nCost functions\n\nEach data point is compared with the master curve built from all other curves (a curve is one size, or one combination of size and ansatz.group values). One of the two cost functions must be chosen:\n\nFixed window (eval_window = (lo, hi)): only points with mathrmlo  x  mathrmhi in the unscaled x are scored, so the scored set does not change with the parameters. Points outside it still serve for interpolation. Where another curve does not reach a point's scaled x, it is extrapolated linearly and penalty  d^2 is added, with d the distance outside in units of that curve's scaled range.\nClassic (classic_cost_function=true): the cost of Houdayer & Hartmann (2004), with a local linear fit through the neighbouring points of the other curves. It scores every point where the curves overlap in the scaled x, so for the smallest sizes it reaches furthest from the transition, where corrections to scaling are largest.\n\nThe scaling form holds as t  0 in the unscaled t, for any scaled x. A window in the unscaled x bounds the corrections that grow with t equally for all sizes, so the fixed window is recommended.\n\nKeywords\n\nuse_all_sizes: also include a point's own curve in its master curve (fixed-window cost only).\nfix: parameters held at fixed values, e.g. fix=(; a=1/ν). They are left out of the arguments of the cost.\n\nData requirements\n\nOnly the columns used by ansatz are read. Rows with missing or non-finite values in them, or with a non-positive error, are skipped with a warning.\nSizes must be positive integers, and there must be at least two of them.\nEach curve needs at least two rows, with distinct x.\n\nAn Autoscaler reuses internal buffers, so one instance must not be evaluated from several threads at once; see Autoscaler(::Autoscaler) for independent copies.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> data = [(; L, x, y=L^-0.25 * tanh(L * (x - 0.3)), y_err=1e-3)\n               for L in (8, 16, 32) for x in 0.2:0.005:0.4];\n\njulia> s = Autoscaler(data; eval_window=(0.28, 0.32));\n\njulia> round(s(1.0, 0.25, 0.3); digits=3)  # a, b, x_c: a good collapse\n0.005\n\njulia> round(s(1.2, 0.25, 0.3); digits=1)  # a bad one\n270.2\n\njulia> s_b = Autoscaler(data; eval_window=(0.28, 0.32), fix=(; b=0.25));\n\njulia> s_b(1.0, 0.3) == s(1.0, 0.25, 0.3)\ntrue\n\nReferences\n\nJ. Houdayer and A. K. Hartmann, Low-temperature behavior of two-dimensional Gaussian Ising spin glasses, Phys. Rev. B 70, 014418 (2004), doi:10.1103/PhysRevB.70.014418.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Autoscalers.Autoscaler"},{"category":"function","location":"#Autoscalers.cost_breakdown","page":"Home","text":"cost_breakdown(a::Autoscaler, args...)\n\nThe fixed-window cost at the free parameters args, split into its parts: a NamedTuple (; cost, chi2, penalty) with cost = chi2 + penalty. Requires eval_window.\n\nA non-negligible penalty means the collapse relies on extrapolating curves beyond their data; shrink eval_window until it vanishes.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> data = [(; L, x, y=L^-0.25 * tanh(L * (x - 0.3)), y_err=1e-3)\n               for L in (8, 16, 32) for x in 0.2:0.005:0.4];\n\njulia> s = Autoscaler(data; eval_window=(0.28, 0.32));\n\njulia> map(c -> round(c; digits=3), cost_breakdown(s, 1.0, 0.25, 0.3))\n(cost = 0.005, chi2 = 0.005, penalty = 0.0)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Autoscalers.cost_breakdown"},{"category":"function","location":"#Autoscalers.to_table","page":"Home","text":"to_table(a::Autoscaler, args...)\n\nThe data scaled with the free parameters args, for plotting the collapse. Returns a NamedTuple of columns, one row per data point:\n\nL: system size;\nthe group columns of the ansatz, if any;\nx, y, y_err: scaled values;\nmaster_curve: the master curve the point is compared to;\nin_window: whether the point is scored by the fixed-window cost.\n\nThe column names are always these, whatever the columns are called in the data. With DataFrames.jl loaded, DataFrame(a, args) returns the same as a DataFrame.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> data = [(; L, x, y=L^-0.25 * tanh(L * (x - 0.3)), y_err=1e-3)\n               for L in (8, 16, 32) for x in 0.2:0.005:0.4];\n\njulia> t = to_table(Autoscaler(data; eval_window=(0.28, 0.32)), 1.0, 0.25, 0.3);\n\njulia> keys(t)\n(:L, :x, :y, :y_err, :master_curve, :in_window)\n\njulia> count(t.in_window)\n27\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Autoscalers.to_table"},{"category":"function","location":"#Autoscalers.fit_collapse","page":"Home","text":"fit_collapse(\n    a::Autoscaler, x0;\n    method=NelderMead(...), options=Optim.Options(...), restarts=1,\n)\n\nMinimise the cost of a over its free parameters with Optim.jl, starting from x0.\n\nReturns a NamedTuple with\n\nparams: the fitted free parameters;\ncost: the cost at params;\nconverged: whether the optimiser converged;\nresult: the Optim result of the last run, for details such as the number of iterations (nothing if all parameters are fixed).\n\nBy default this uses the Nelder–Mead simplex method with a small initial simplex around x0. It is restarted restarts times from the previous minimum, because a single run can stall in an elongated simplex. Any derivative-free Optim method and options can be passed instead.\n\nWith the fixed-window cost, a warning is shown if the fitted collapse extrapolates curves beyond their data, i.e. its extrapolation penalty is not zero. It suggests the widest part of eval_window around its centre that needs no extrapolation.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> data = [(; L, x, y=L^-0.25 * tanh(L * (x - 0.3)), y_err=1e-3)\n               for L in (8, 16, 32) for x in 0.2:0.005:0.4];\n\njulia> fit = fit_collapse(Autoscaler(data; eval_window=(0.28, 0.32)), [1.1, 0.2, 0.31]);\n\njulia> fit.converged\ntrue\n\njulia> round.(fit.params; digits=3)  # a, b, x_c\n3-element Vector{Float64}:\n 0.999\n 0.249\n 0.3\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Autoscalers.fit_collapse"},{"category":"function","location":"#Autoscalers.bootstrap","page":"Home","text":"bootstrap(\n    a::Autoscaler, estimate;\n    n=1000, rng=Random.default_rng(), parallel=true, progress=isinteractive(),\n    method=NelderMead(...), options=Optim.Options(...),\n)\n\nParametric bootstrap (Efron & Tibshirani, 1993; Davison & Hinkley, 1997) of the free parameters around their fitted values estimate. Returns a BootstrapResult, for use with confint, stderror and pvalue.\n\nThe fitted collapse is taken as the true model:\n\nAt estimate, the master curve at each point is the error-weighted mean of all curves covering it. The ansatz is inverted to map it back to the data, for the first column with errors.\nEach of the n replicates adds Gaussian noise of the measured errors to that model (and to the other columns with errors).\nEach replicate is refitted with fit_collapse, using method and options, starting from estimate.\n\nIf any refit extrapolates curves beyond their data (see fit_collapse), a single warning reports how many did.\n\nThe spread of the refitted parameters estimates the statistical error only. Systematic errors, for example from the choice of eval_window or corrections to scaling, are not included.\n\nKeywords\n\nn: number of replicates.\nrng: random number generator. The results are reproducible for a given rng, whatever the number of threads.\nparallel: run the replicates on all Julia threads (start Julia with --threads).\nprogress: show a progress bar (by default in interactive sessions).\nmethod, options: passed to fit_collapse.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> using Random\n\njulia> data = [(; L, x, y=L^-0.25 * tanh(L * (x - 0.3)), y_err=1e-3)\n               for L in (8, 16, 32) for x in 0.2:0.005:0.4];\n\njulia> s = Autoscaler(data; eval_window=(0.28, 0.32));\n\njulia> fit = fit_collapse(s, [1.1, 0.2, 0.31]);\n\njulia> b = bootstrap(s, fit.params; n=200, rng=Xoshiro(1));\n\njulia> b\nBootstrapResult(a, b, x_c; 200/200 replicates converged)\n\njulia> round.(confint(b).a; digits=2)\n(0.92, 1.05)\n\nReferences\n\nB. Efron and R. J. Tibshirani, An Introduction to the Bootstrap, Chapman & Hall (1993), doi:10.1201/9780429246593 (CRC reprint).\nA. C. Davison and D. V. Hinkley, Bootstrap Methods and their Application, Cambridge University Press (1997), doi:10.1017/CBO9780511802843.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Autoscalers.bootstrap"},{"category":"type","location":"#Autoscalers.BootstrapResult","page":"Home","text":"BootstrapResult\n\nResult of bootstrap. Use confint, stderror and pvalue on it; these use only the replicates whose fit converged.\n\nFields\n\nestimate: the free parameters the bootstrap was run at.\nsamples: the refitted free parameters, one row per replicate and one column per parameter.\nconverged: whether each replicate's fit converged.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Autoscalers.BootstrapResult"},{"category":"method","location":"#StatsAPI.confint-Tuple{BootstrapResult}","page":"Home","text":"confint(b::BootstrapResult; level=0.95)\n\nBias-corrected percentile (BC) confidence intervals (Efron, 1987) of every free parameter, as a NamedTuple of (lower, upper).\n\nThe intervals follow the shape of the bootstrap distribution, so they can be asymmetric around the estimate. They need no extra fits, and on synthetic collapses they are close to their nominal coverage (see test/scripts/bootstrap_coverage.jl).\n\nMethod\n\nWith level = 1 - α, the bounds are the quantiles of the samples at the levels Φ(2z_0  z_α2), where Φ is the standard normal distribution function and z_0 = Φ^-1(P(θ^*  hatθ)) measures how far the bootstrap distribution is off-centre from the estimate.\n\nFor a centred distribution (z_0 = 0), this is the plain percentile interval.\nA large z_0 moves the levels towards 0 or 1, where quantiles need many replicates (n gtrsim 1000).\n\nOther intervals can be computed from b.samples, using the rows marked in b.converged.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> using Random\n\njulia> data = [(; L, x, y=L^-0.25 * tanh(L * (x - 0.3)), y_err=1e-3)\n               for L in (8, 16, 32) for x in 0.2:0.005:0.4];\n\njulia> s = Autoscaler(data; eval_window=(0.28, 0.32));\n\njulia> fit = fit_collapse(s, [1.1, 0.2, 0.31]);\n\njulia> b = bootstrap(s, fit.params; n=200, rng=Xoshiro(1));\n\njulia> round.(confint(b).a; digits=2)\n(0.92, 1.05)\n\njulia> round.(confint(b; level=0.68).a; digits=2)\n(0.95, 1.02)\n\nReferences\n\nB. Efron, Better bootstrap confidence intervals, J. Am. Stat. Assoc. 82, 171 (1987), doi:10.1080/01621459.1987.10478410.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"StatsAPI.confint"},{"category":"method","location":"#StatsAPI.stderror-Tuple{BootstrapResult}","page":"Home","text":"stderror(b::BootstrapResult)\n\nBootstrap standard errors of every free parameter, as a NamedTuple: the standard deviation of the samples. Needs at least two converged replicates. For asymmetric errors, prefer confint.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> using Random\n\njulia> data = [(; L, x, y=L^-0.25 * tanh(L * (x - 0.3)), y_err=1e-3)\n               for L in (8, 16, 32) for x in 0.2:0.005:0.4];\n\njulia> s = Autoscaler(data; eval_window=(0.28, 0.32));\n\njulia> fit = fit_collapse(s, [1.1, 0.2, 0.31]);\n\njulia> b = bootstrap(s, fit.params; n=200, rng=Xoshiro(1));\n\njulia> round(stderror(b).a; digits=3)\n0.036\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"StatsAPI.stderror"},{"category":"method","location":"#StatsAPI.pvalue-Tuple{BootstrapResult, Vararg{Pair{Symbol, <:Real}}}","page":"Home","text":"pvalue(b::BootstrapResult, name => value...; method=:bc, kwargs...)\n\nTwo-sided p-value of the hypothesis that the parameters name equal value.\n\nMethods\n\nmethod=:bc (default, no extra fits) tests one parameter. It is the test dual to the BC interval of confint (Efron, 1987): p  α exactly when value lies outside the interval of level = 1 - α, up to the resolution of the replicates.\n\nThe fractions P of samples below and above value, with the +1 correction (Davison & Hinkley, 1997, see bootstrap), are mapped through Φ(Φ^-1(P)  2z_0), and p is twice the smaller one.\nOn the side towards which the BC interval is stretched, the smallest attainable p-value is 2Φ(Φ^-1(1(n + 1)) + 2z_0): about 0.005 for n = 1000 and z_0 = 015, but 0.06 for z_0 = 06. To reject strongly off-centre cases, use more replicates or method=:null.\n\nmethod=:null follows the first guideline of Hall & Wilson (1991): it resamples under the hypothesis.\n\nThe parameters are refitted with name fixed at value, and the replicates are drawn from that constrained collapse.\nEach replicate is fitted with and without the constraint. p is the fraction of replicates whose cost increase from the constraint is at least the observed one (with the +1 correction).\nIt can test several parameters at once and needs 2n fits.\nThe observed cost increase is measured from b.estimate, so that should be the minimum found by fit_collapse. A rounded or hand-picked estimate has a higher cost, which makes the observed increase too small and p too large.\nThe keywords n (default: the number of replicates of b), rng, parallel and progress are as for bootstrap. The refits use the optimiser of b (b.method and b.options, as given to bootstrap); the keyword method of pvalue only chooses the test.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> using Random\n\njulia> data = [(; L, x, y=L^-0.25 * tanh(L * (x - 0.3)), y_err=1e-3)\n               for L in (8, 16, 32) for x in 0.2:0.005:0.4];\n\njulia> s = Autoscaler(data; eval_window=(0.28, 0.32));\n\njulia> fit = fit_collapse(s, [1.1, 0.2, 0.31]);\n\njulia> b = bootstrap(s, fit.params; n=200, rng=Xoshiro(1));\n\njulia> round(pvalue(b, :a => 1.0); digits=2)  # the true value\n0.86\n\njulia> round(pvalue(b, :a => 1.1); digits=3)\n0.004\n\nReferences\n\nB. Efron, Better bootstrap confidence intervals, J. Am. Stat. Assoc. 82, 171 (1987), doi:10.1080/01621459.1987.10478410.\nP. Hall and S. R. Wilson, Two guidelines for bootstrap hypothesis testing, Biometrics 47, 757 (1991), doi:10.2307/2532163.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"StatsAPI.pvalue"}]
}
